 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Eye Artifacts:** Eyes may appear uncanny or blurry. This occurs because training data predominantly shows subjects looking at the camera. The GAN's NeRF renderer learns to align pupils with the camera angle. Since the baseline decoder disables spherical harmonics (view-dependence), it learns an averaged, forward-looking eye [S1].
+*   **Eye Artifacts:** Eyes may appear uncanny or blurry [S1]. This occurs because training data predominantly shows subjects looking at the camera [S1]. The GAN's NeRF renderer learns to align pupils with the camera angle [S1]. Since the baseline decoder disables spherical harmonics (view-dependence), it learns an averaged, forward-looking eye [S1].
 *   **Grid Artifacts:** Using uniform 3D grid sampling for position initialization causes visible line artifacts on surfaces, reducing realism despite good quantitative metrics [S1].
-*   **Loss Imbalance:** Disabling LPIPS leads to blurriness; disabling L1/SSIM leads to edge artifacts. The baseline combines all losses to maintain a balanced tradeoff across metrics [S1].
